# Two live orders share one entry in the FPGA's order table.
#
# The order table has 4,096 entries, picked by the low 12 bits of the
# exchange's order ref. An order that rests while 4,096 newer orders are
# entered loses its entry to one of them. Its shares are still correctly in
# the book; the trouble starts when the feed later changes it and the table
# can't find it. Then the book can't be updated correctly, so the FPGA halts:
# it cancels both quotes and stops quoting.
#
# Participants: 2 and 3 provide the book, 4 rests one deep order, 5 churns.

latency market_data 5_000
latency order_entry 10_000
timer 1_000_000
end 20_000_000

0            2 enter 1 B 500 100.00
0            3 enter 1 S 500 100.01
0            4 enter 1 B 100 99.90
0            5 enter 1 B 100 99.50

# Participant 5 replaces its order 4,200 times, 1 us apart, at the same price
# and size. Each replacement gets a new ref, so the refs pass participant 4's
# ref + 4,096 and one lands on its table entry. Nothing visible changes: no
# orders. (These lines are generated.)
1_001_000    5 replace 1 2 100 99.50
1_002_000    5 replace 2 3 100 99.50
1_003_000    5 replace 3 4 100 99.50
1_004_000    5 replace 4 5 100 99.50
1_005_000    5 replace 5 6 100 99.50
